<p class="lead">Ready to hunt compound bow packages promise a simple deal: one box, one price, and a bow that&rsquo;s set up with the accessories you need to start shooting. For a new archer, that&rsquo;s a huge head start. But not every package is equal, and the difference between a bow you love for years and one you outgrow in a season usually comes down to a handful of features.</p>

<p>This Better Outdoors guide isn&rsquo;t a ranked list built on made-up test scores. It&rsquo;s a buying checklist. You&rsquo;ll learn what to look for in an RTH bow, how factory, dealer-built and bare-bow options compare, and how real packages we&rsquo;ve already reviewed stack up on paper.</p>

<?= tool_cta('bow-finder') ?>

<h2 id="h-what-is-a-ready-to-hunt-compound-bow-package">What Is a Ready to Hunt Compound Bow Package?</h2>
<p>A ready-to-hunt (RTH) package is a compound bow sold with its core accessories already included and, in most cases, installed. A typical RTH bow ships with:</p>
<ul class="checks">
  <li>A multi-pin sight</li>
  <li>An arrow rest, often a full-containment style</li>
  <li>A bow-mounted quiver</li>
  <li>A short stabilizer</li>
  <li>A peep sight served into the string</li>
  <li>A D-loop for your release</li>
</ul>
<p>What you usually still need: arrows, field points, broadheads, a release aid and a target. Some retailers bundle arrows; most don&rsquo;t. Read the listing carefully so you know what&rsquo;s in the box.</p>

<h2 id="h-beginner-compound-bow-package-checklist">Beginner Compound Bow Package Checklist: Features That Matter</h2>
<p>So, what features should you look for in a beginner compound bow package? Start with the bow itself, because accessories are easy to upgrade later and the bow is not. Use this checklist as you compare listings.</p>
