<h3 id="h-draw-length-and-draw-weight-adjustability">Draw Length and Draw Weight Adjustability</h3>
<p>This is the most important line on the spec sheet. Measure your draw length before you shop, and make sure the bow&rsquo;s range covers it comfortably. If you&rsquo;re still growing, or buying for a teen, a wider range is worth more than any accessory. Learn how module changes work in our guide on <?= post_link('how-to-adjust-draw-length', 'how to adjust draw length') ?>.</p>
<p>Draw weight matters just as much. Many adult hunting bows come in two peak ranges, such as 45&ndash;60 lb or 55&ndash;70 lb. A beginner who isn&rsquo;t sure should usually pick the lower range, because you can shoot it comfortably now with good form. Check your state&rsquo;s bowhunting regulations for any minimum draw weight.</p>
<?= tool_cta('draw-length-calculator') ?>

<h3 id="h-axle-to-axle-brace-height-and-let-off">Axle-to-Axle, Brace Height and Let-Off</h3>
<p>Axle-to-axle (ATA) length affects maneuverability and steadiness. Treestand and ground blind hunters often like something in the low 30s. Brace height is the distance from the grip to the string at rest. A brace height around 6 to 7 inches is generally more forgiving than the short-brace speed bows, which helps new shooters.</p>
<p>Let-off is the percentage of peak weight that drops off at full draw. With 80% let-off on a 60-lb bow, you hold about 12 lb. Some cams allow you to change let-off with the draw stop, which is a nice bonus.</p>

<h3 id="h-sight-rest-quiver-and-stabilizer">Sight, Rest, Quiver and Stabilizer</h3>
<p>Package accessories are where manufacturers save money, so look closely:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Sight:</strong> Look for fiber optic pins, a level and micro-adjustment if possible. Three to five pins handle most whitetail distances.</li>
  <li><strong>Rest:</strong> Full-containment rests are quiet and nearly foolproof for beginners. Drop-away rests offer more clearance but need careful timing.</li>
  <li><strong>Quiver:</strong> It should hold arrows tightly without rattling and detach easily for stand hunting.</li>
  <li><strong>Stabilizer:</strong> A short hunting stabilizer adds balance and soaks up vibration. It doesn&rsquo;t need to be fancy.</li>
</ul>
<?= callout('tip', 'Upgrade later, fit first', 'Don&rsquo;t pass on a bow that fits you perfectly because the package sight is basic. A sight or rest is a simple swap. Draw length range, brace height and ATA are permanent.') ?>

<h2 id="h-rth-vs-dealer-built-vs-bare-bow">RTH Bow vs. Dealer-Built Package vs. Bare Bow</h2>
<p>There are three ways to end up with a hunting-ready compound bow. Each has a place.</p>
